{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/jyx0ra1s","slideIndex":3,"text":"Finally, we wanted to visualize how hand movement changes over time.\nThis graph shows average lateral hand velocity for Participant A in Condition A, using slow-target trials from all three blocks.\nWe aligned every trial to the Pixel event, shown by the dashed vertical line at zero milliseconds, and calculated the average velocity at each time point for the three background categories.\nBefore the event, all three categories follow relatively similar patterns, with velocity increasing toward zero.\nAfter the event, velocity continues increasing, but the curves begin to diverge later in the movement.\nThis shows that there are differences in average movement trajectories across background categories.\nWe also examined acceleration, which will be particularly important for estimating corrective movement onset. However, those signals were much noisier, and the average did not show a clear corrective response.\nBefore proceeding to modeling, we'll need to clarify how leftward and rightward corrective movements are identified so that we can properly preprocess and interpret the acceleration data.\n"}
